<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Varman dynasty of Bengal</h1><p class="st-meta">1050 CE – 1150 CE (approx.) · capital Vikramapura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A dynasty of eastern Bengal ruling from Vikramapura in the late 11th and early 12th centuries, between the Chandras and the Senas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Varmans claimed descent from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> family of Simhapura, probably in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kalinga">Kalinga</a>. Jatavarman, who married a princess of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalachuris-of-tripuri">Kalachuris of Tripuri</a>, established their rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanga">Vanga</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire</a> weakened in the late 11th century, after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chandra-dynasty">Chandra dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Varmans ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramapura">Vikramapura</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> and were patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanism">Brahmanical Hinduism</a>. Harivarman's minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhavadeva-bhatta">Bhavadeva Bhatta</a> was a noted scholar of Vedic ritual and law. The dynasty is known from a small number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates, such as the Belava plate of Bhojavarman, and from Bhavadeva's inscription.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayasena">Vijayasena</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sena-dynasty">Sena dynasty</a> took eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, ending Varman rule.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1050 CE – 1150 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.60%;width:1.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1050 CE – 1150 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vikramapura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jatavarman, Harivarman, Bhojavarman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Proto-Bengali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Bangladesh</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Varmans of Vanga</dd></dl></aside></div>
